the united states is battling the  largest measles outbreak in decades  the rapid spread of measles is prompting  newer and stronger measures from health  officials across the country  the latest outbreaks are highlighting  pockets of unvaccinated people  communities across the country are  taking drastic measures  amid the backdrop of the current  outbreak if you go online you might see  false information about vaccines  information that science is debunked for  years like this the spread of  misinformation can affect something like  a measles outbreak or you know attitudes  about information in general of course  because it's affecting the way that  people are getting their information  it's affecting what they believe so far  there are no large-scale studies that  say that vaccine misinformation causes  outbreaks but social media companies are  starting to make changes to their core  products in an effort to tamp down on  these kinds of posts changes that some  critics say aren't enough here's a look  at what they're doing some are making it  harder for users to search for false  content Facebook Instagram and Pinterest  are relying on information from global  health organizations to help them  identify false information and make it  harder to find for example  the Centers for Disease Control and  Prevention has confirmed that there's no  link between getting the measles vaccine  and developing autism Facebook takes  this kind of information and uses a  combination of Technology and human  reviewers to identify groups that  suggest that there is a link once a page  gets flagged Facebook's algorithm makes  it harder to find  for example they would likely flag we  know vaccines cause autism 1 2 3 a group  we made up for the purpose of this video  once flagged the page would appear lower  in the newsfeed it would also be reduced  in search results and removed from  search recommendations for Facebook the  system is only in place for pages that  make explicitly false claims as in  claims that are proven to be  scientifically incorrect that means many  groups that use anti vaccine language  but don't include a specifically false  claim might not get flagged this made-up  group for example might not get flagged  even though it's promoting anti vaccine  opinions  you'll get is a short-term fix for again  probably those things that have gained  the most publicity but that doesn't mean  that there's that that there is  something fundamentally changed about  the way that the platform is operating  or in many cases the kind of content  that you can find in response Facebook  cites research that complements previous  findings that shows how the overall  consumption of false news on Facebook  has declined since the 2016 u.s.  elections other platforms like Instagram  and Pinterest have made moves to stop  spreading misinformation by hiding  results for false hashtags now when you  search for hashtag vaccines cause autism  on Instagram or hashtag vaccines cause  SIDS on Pinterest nothing pops up but  similar to Facebook opinion hashtags  won't be banned on Instagram so if you  search for hashtag vaccines are bad  searches will still come up and even  some hashtags that are false like  hashtag vaccines cause autism read the  studies still pop up Instagram says it's  constantly trying to get ahead of  permutations of the hashtag and  vernacular that they think still  infringes upon their misinformation  policies they say they view their  efforts to combat misinformation more  broadly saying they're not problems you  can fix but rather issues you can  continually improve on the tools that  can operate at the scale that these  platforms need do not have the  sophistication and the nuance to be able  to distinguish between something that is  you know definitely misinformation and  something that definitely isn't and so  what you tend to get in these solutions  are these kind of these kind of  whack-a-mole approaches right where one  set of search results will be black  blocked or ones hashtag will be blocked  because that's been flagged as  problematic YouTube is also trying to  counteract misinformation by giving  users information from third-party  sources like Wikipedia and Encyclopedia  Britannica the aim is to guide users to  more sources where they can fact check  information for themselves for example  look at this trailer for a documentary  that claims to educate parents around  the world about what it calls vaccine  injury just below the video there's a  link to the Wikipedia page for vaccine  controversies the first sentence of the  page explains how the world health  organization identified vaccine  hesitancy as one of the top 10 global  threats of 2019 whatsapp is also working  to fight against the spread of  misinformation by changing its rules on  forwarding in India anti vaccine  misinformation is spreading on the  messaging service making it harder to  root out measles and rubella in a  country where tens of thousands of  people are struck by the disease's every  year and the story is one of the biggest  examples of how spreading fake news  could lead to massive social and  political problems users are sending  around messages like this one that says  in Hindi and urdu don't use vaccinations  save the lives of your children whatsapp  has recently made product changes to  fight against misinformation of all  kinds not just related to health  belike Kavya shed joy not rumors before  whatsapp users could forward a message  to over a hundred accounts at the same  time but as of January 2019 whatsapp  tweaked its rules we're now you can only  forward a message to up to five chats  globally are you really sure that this  is true no man it was a followed they've  also started marking which messages had  been forwarded by a user rather than  sent directly for example this message  with the forwarded tag comes from a  third party the messaging service also  allows users to report accounts by  clicking here if whatsapp's automated  system recognizes that an account has  been reported many times they may ban it  whatsapp says it bans nearly two million  accounts per month but while the company  bans accounts that many users have  reported they don't ban accounts based  on reports about the content of messages  so the account that sent the message  from before won't necessarily be banned  so as these companies introduce new  rules to fight against the spread of  vaccine misinformation there's one  common theme none of them offer a  blanket solution owning it means one  taking responsibility for the editorial  choices that they are making and where  they fall short and also maybe  acknowledging that if they're that if  their tools can't keep up with the  volume of information flowing across the  platform maybe that much information  shouldn't be flowing across the platform  in response Pinterest has said that much  of their content comes onto their site  from other platforms that don't have a  pall  we'll see against health misinformation  which is why this is an industry-wide  issue that will only improve if more  companies recognize the effectiveness of  vaccines like measles mumps and rubella  as settled science YouTube says their  changes are still taking effect but a  very preliminary look shows that it is  limiting the spread of borderline  content and harmful misinformation on  their platform and whatsapp has made it  clear when users have received forwarded  information and limited for words to  reduce the spread of viral content all  of the company's mentions say they're  working on longer-term fixes to solve  the problem  Facebook is planning to add warning  signs on pages and groups that are known  to spread false information so this  made-up group would likely have a symbol  or warning sign pop up beside the page  as these companies continue to employ  these rules to fight against health  misinformation the measles outbreak  continues to grow
 

